C1M Churn Benchmark Result - 2026-08-24 (fixed cwist handshake shepherd)
Methodology: 20 h2load processes x (-c 5000 -n 50000 -r 1000 -T 30) =
100,000 concurrently held TLS connections, 1,000,000 requests total.
See run_c1m_bench.sh header for why "1M held TLS connections" is not the
target for the thread-parking HTTPS path.

RESULT: COMPLETED — no stall.

  Total Requests:     1,000,000
  Total Succeeded:    756,610 (75.7%)
  Total Errored:      243,390 (client-side -T 30 recycles of slow conns)
  Wall time:          ~7 min (per-process "finished in" 410-425 s)
  Phantom connections: 0 (server-side and client-side ESTABLISHED counts
                         matched for the whole run)

  Previously (2026-08-23, pre-fix): the same load deadlocked — ramp to
  ~85k concurrent connections, then zero progress until the watchdog
  killed the clients.  cwist fix: the TLS handshake moved out of the
  worker pool into a non-blocking shepherd thread
  (cwist "perf(https): non-blocking TLS handshake shepherd"), removing
  the worker-parking collapse that starved the accept loop and produced
  phantom connections.

  Remaining errors are throughput-related, not correctness: with the
  shepherd doing all handshake crypto, ~24% of connections exceeded the
  h2load-side 30 s active timeout during bursts and were recycled by the
  client.  Server stayed responsive throughout and shut down cleanly.

